{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgaeG8PCernMVseJYF5fu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I proved `sumAcc_zero` in `Main.lean`, and `lean Main.lean` now finishes with no errors or warnings.\n\nInduction directly on `sumAcc_zero` doesn't work, because the accumulator stops being `0` after the first step. So I added a more general helper lemma first:\n\n```lean\ntheorem sumAcc_eq (xs : List Nat) (acc : Nat) : sumAcc xs acc = acc + mySum xs := by\n  induction xs generalizing acc with\n  | nil => simp [sumAcc, mySum]\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [sumAcc, mySum, ih, Nat.add_assoc]\n\ntheorem sumAcc_zero (xs : List Nat) : sumAcc xs 0 = mySum xs := by\n  simp [sumAcc_eq]\n```\n\n- **Helper lemma:** `sumAcc_eq` says that running `sumAcc` with any starting value `acc` gives `acc + mySum xs`. It's proved by induction on the list, and `generalizing acc` lets the induction step use a different accumulator value.\n- **Original theorem:** `sumAcc_zero` is the case `acc = 0`, and `simp` reduces `0 + mySum xs` to `mySum xs`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":614,"cache_read_input_tokens":7926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":614},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c05dc6bc-e9f8-44ec-a817-dc4da6b8d392","uuid":"baa97743-8ab0-4eca-8dce-e02a8d8ebd47","timestamp":"2026-10-31T16:38:10.601Z","request_id":"req_011CgaeG837yn2TCLkrfNPWC"}
